+# Quantitative-methods prepass findings
+
+**Issue:** MHAC-R002 / #2. **Assessed main:** `796151a33ec7a1acc026b239fe5c883312ebf40b`. **Status:** analytical contribution, not protocol freeze, empirical synthesis, superiority ranking or #1 infrastructure acceptance. The 14-row decision table, extraction contract and proposed clauses accompany this report. Sources and exact reading limits are in SOURCES.md.
+
+## What remains to be decided, and what this prepass resolves
+
+The current synthesis instructions already reject pooling incompatible constructs and double-counting samples. They do not yet identify a quantitative target, selection hierarchy, covariance procedure or operational non-pooling rule. This prepass proposes those rules without revisiting the access status of the existing auditory-alert review. Prior methodology packets were not available locally; retrieved summaries were used only to identify the deferred statistical scope. No empirical audio-effect estimates informed the fixtures or policy choices.
+
+The main proposed decision is to organize synthesis around the **question actually estimated**, not the sound-family label or the availability of an effect size. A short sound can yield a quick recognition response, incorrect action, expensive training and poor retention simultaneously. Those are separate questions. A project-level preference or a broad term such as comprehension cannot make their standardized effects commensurable.
+
+## Outcome questions that must remain distinct
+
+| Family | Question to identify | Important non-equivalence |
+|---|---|---|
+| Recognition | Does the listener correctly map the message after the stated exposure? | Detection, first-attempt recognition, success after replay and action execution are not the same endpoint. |
+| Action | Is the required response appropriate, within the relevant deadline and allowed repair policy? | A label-selection keypress does not by itself establish situated action; wrong, omitted and late actions matter. |
+| Latency | How long until which event, from which clock origin, among which responses? | Correct-only RT, all-response RT and time to eventual correction select different outcomes/populations. |
+| Training | What performance follows a fixed training budget, or what budget is needed for mastery? | Equal training dose versus equal final mastery answers different questions; unsuccessful learners cannot disappear. |
+| Retention | What persists after a specified delay and intervening exposure? | Delayed level, decrement from immediate performance and relearning burden are distinct. |
+| Workload | What subjectively rated demand/effort occurs under a specified task and instrument? | Preference, annoyance, perceived urgency and observed errors are not workload proxies; TLX overall/subscales/scoring differ. |
+
+These are original operational proposals informed by the estimand framework [M08], synthesis grouping guidance [M03], speed–accuracy methods [M11], and the instrument owner's workload definition [M10]. No universal audio-family ordering follows from them.
+
+A useful synthesis key is `(population, task/semantics, A:C interface, exposure/training, outcome/event, timing, failure/repair policy, summary/scale, analysis unit, design constraints)`. Whether mean latency includes the acoustic message itself is especially important for brief interfaces: subtracting sound duration changes the question from end-to-end response burden to post-stimulus processing, and cannot automatically be done for every observation. Likewise an A:C comparison with different wording and training is a package comparison, not an isolated acoustic-style effect.
+
+## Two correlations with opposite roles
+
+For n paired participants, let A and C be participant-level measurements. A raw mean difference has
+
+`Var(mean(A-C)) = (sA² + sC² - 2 r_person sA sC) / n`.
+
+By contrast, for estimated contrasts y and prespecified weights a,
+
+`Var(a'y) = a' V a = sum(a_i² Var(y_i)) + 2 sum(i<j)(a_i a_j Cov(y_i,y_j))`.
+
+Positive pairing may reduce a contrast SE, while positive correlation between contrasts increases an average's SE. Treating either correlation as the other is a substantive error. A pairing correlation cannot generally populate the contrast-covariance matrix. [M01, M02; algebraic application]
+
+When A, B and C are independent arm means, `Cov(A-C, B-C)=Var(C)`. When the same participants receive all conditions, it becomes `Cov(A,B)-Cov(A,C)-Cov(C,B)+Var(C)`. More generally, with condition covariance Sigma and contrast matrix C, `V = C Sigma C'`. This linear formula applies to raw mean/linear contrasts; nonlinear effect measures such as log odds ratios or standardized mean differences need their own delta-method/model covariance, not this raw-MD formula copied unchanged. Contrast orientation changes covariance signs. A-B equals (A-C)-(B-C), so retaining all three does not create a third independent contrast. Exact redundancy may make V singular; use an identified contrast basis or aggregate, not an arbitrary numerical ridge.
+
+## Worked synthetic example: precision lost to false independence
+
+The fixture is authored summary data, **not real participants or a simulation calibrated from audio evidence**. It stipulates three disjoint sample IDs and three interchangeable cue exemplars within each. Each contrast is an A-minus-C first-attempt recognition difference in percentage points, with supplied SE=2. There is no claim that those SEs were estimated from the nominal participant counts.
+
+| Synthetic sample | Cue 1 | Cue 2 | Cue 3 | Equal-cue sample mean |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| S1 | 4 | 5 | 6 | 5 |
+| S2 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 2 |
+| S3 | -2 | -1 | 0 | -1 |
+
+The illustrative target is the equal-weight average of these **three specified sample-population effects**, each with an equal mixture of these three cues. It is a finite target, not the mean of a superpopulation of studies. Equal sample and cue counts keep the point estimate at 2, isolating the precision error. Different sample effects do not invalidate a fixed finite weighted target; they do invalidate unqualified generalization to a common universal effect.
+
+The deliberately incorrect independence calculation uses nine independent observations:
+
+`mean = 18/9 = 2; Var_naive = 9×4 / 9² = 4/9; SE_naive = 0.666667`.
+
+Under a stipulated within-sample compound-symmetric *contrast* correlation rho, each 3×3 block has diagonal 4 and off-diagonal 4rho. For one sample:
+
+`Var(sample mean) = (3×4 + 6×4rho)/9 = (4/3)(1+2rho)`.
+
+For the average of three independent sample means:
+
+`Var(target estimate) = (4/9)(1+2rho)`.
+
+Thus rho=.6 gives `Var=44/45=0.977778`, `SE=0.988826`. False independence gives **2.2 times too much inverse-variance precision**, or an SE that must increase by **48.3%** to account for this stipulated covariance. The phrase “48.3%” compares corrected SE with naive SE; equivalently the naive SE is 32.6% smaller than the corrected SE.
+
+| Assumed contrast rho | SE | Conditional normal 95% interval | Correct variance / naive variance |
+|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| -0.25 | 0.4714 | 1.0761 to 2.9239 | 0.5 |
+| 0 | 0.6667 | 0.6934 to 3.3066 | 1.0 |
+| 0.25 | 0.8165 | 0.3997 to 3.6003 | 1.5 |
+| 0.50 | 0.9428 | 0.1521 to 3.8479 | 2.0 |
+| 0.60 | 0.9888 | 0.0619 to 3.9381 | 2.2 |
+| 0.75 | 1.0541 | -0.0660 to 4.0660 | 2.5 |
+| 0.90 | 1.1155 | -0.1864 to 4.1864 | 2.8 |
+| 1.00 | 1.1547 | -0.2632 to 4.2632 | 3.0 |
+
+All intervals here use the normal quantile and **condition on the supplied variances and the chosen rho**. They are not random-effects intervals, account for neither variance-estimation uncertainty nor study-population sampling, and cannot validate small-k HKSJ/RVE performance. Whether an illustrative interval crosses zero is not an empirical finding or a correlation-selection rule. No one rho is asserted to be the unknown true value.
+
+For a three-contrast equicorrelation matrix, mathematical feasibility requires `-1/2 <= rho <= 1`. The negative scenario is a stress test, not a substantive assertion about expected audio data. Real designs require source-informed bounds and a valid covariance matrix; arbitrary pairwise correlations need not form one. The singular endpoints can still be aggregated because no matrix inverse is used.
+
+## Non-pooled alternative and repeated reports
+
+`synthetic-results/NONPOOLED.md` and `nonpooled.csv` display all nine contrasts with their marginal intervals, grouped by sample, with no overall diamond or vote count. Each row has SE=2 and interval estimate ±3.919928. This makes the S1/S2/S3 differences visible instead of allowing a headline mean to conceal them. Nominal sample sizes are repeated labels, not additive independent n within a sample.
+
+The raw fixture also includes three identical results from a second report of S1. A deliberately incorrect 12-row analysis yields `mean=2.75`, `SE=sqrt(1/3)=0.577350`: a duplicated favourable sample changes both weighting and apparent precision. Canonicalization removes exactly those three repeated-result rows, restoring nine results and three sample identities. A conflicting repeated result is rejected, not averaged. This is a minimal test of result identity, not a replacement for source-grounded publication/sample reconciliation. [M06]
+
+## Second synthetic design: shared independent-arm control
+
+A, B and C have independent n=20 arms, SD=100 ms, and means 620, 640 and 650 ms. Each arm mean variance is 500. Contrasts A-C=-30 and B-C=-10 each have variance 1000, but their covariance is 500 because they reuse C.
+
+The equally weighted contrast-mixture estimate is -20 ms. False independence gives variance `(1000+1000)/4=500`, SE=22.3607. The correct covariance-aware variance is `(1000+1000+2×500)/4=750`, SE=27.3861. This target is a deliberately defined 50:50 mixture of A:C and B:C effects; it is not legitimate merely because the two arms both happen to be called non-speech. Here rho=.5 is derived from the stipulated arm design, not guessed.
+
+## Third synthetic design: missing pairing correlation
+
+The separate paired-RT fixture has n=25, means 620 and 650 ms and SDs 100 and 110 ms. Its mean difference is -30 ms throughout. At within-person r=0, its variance is 884 and SE=29.7321; at r=.5, variance=444 and SE=21.0713; at r=.9, variance=92 and SE=9.5917. A negative stress scenario and additional positive values are in the CSV. These are different from the main contrast-rho grid. Recovering a reported paired contrast SE would eliminate this particular missing-correlation problem; there is no reason to impute r again once that SE is available. [M02]
+
+## Fourth synthetic design: paired binary margins do not identify precision
+
+For one binary result per paired participant, the 40-person joint table is: both correct a=24; A-only correct b=10; C-only correct c=2; neither correct d=4. Marginal success probabilities are .85 and .65, giving RD=.20. For D in {-1,0,1}, the unbiased sample-variance estimate of the mean difference is
+
+`Var(RD) = [ (b+c)/n - RD² ] / (n-1) = (.30-.04)/39 = 1/150`.
+
+SE=0.081650, or 8.165 percentage points. The script independently checks this formula by enumerating the 40 **synthetic** difference values; it is not trial-level evidence.
+
+Keeping only the same margins, possible values of a run from 20 to 26. Enumerating all seven feasible joint tables leaves RD=.20 but changes variance from `.16/39` to `.46/39`, and SE from **0.064051 to 0.108604**. These are logical joint-table bounds, not a 95% interval or a probability distribution over correlations. The raw margins alone do not identify which SE is correct. The simple normal interval is an algebra demonstration; sparse real paired outcomes may require a different inferential procedure. Marginal and matched conditional odds ratios also target different summaries and should not be silently substituted.
+
+## Retention/change covariance and unit-of-analysis risks
+
+A difference in retention changes, `(A_delayed-A_immediate)-(C_delayed-C_immediate)`, has contrast vector `(1,-1,-1,1)` if the measurement order follows that expression. Its variance is c'Sigma c, involving all relevant repeated-measurement covariances. A single pre/post correlation cannot generally identify it. Reported design-valid contrast SEs are valuable; otherwise show dependence sensitivity or keep delayed levels and changes separated.
+
+Trials, cues, blocks and reports can all multiply rows while leaving the number of independent participants unchanged. With unequal trial counts, participant-average accuracy differs from a trial-weighted mean. With a fixed small stimulus set, a participant-only SE does not establish generalization to a new population of sound exemplars. The extraction must retain item identity and generalization scope rather than pretending the nominal trial count addresses both sources of variation. [M01; application to audio tasks]
+
+## What to pool, and what not to pool
+
+Use the 14-row DECISIONS/JSON/CSV table for implementation. First establish a meaningful synthesis cell; next resolve duplicate and overlap identities; next identify the effect and design-valid uncertainty; then use a prespecified single result, a justified covariance-aware aggregate or an adequate multivariate model. Missing variance excludes a result from inverse-variance pooling, not from the review. Uncertain overlap cannot be repaired by relabelling publications as independent clusters.
+
+A random intercept represents variation in true effects; it does not reconstruct omitted sampling covariance from shared participants or controls. A future working V plus `rma.mv` and CR2/Satterthwaite inference is a possible route, subject to enough independent overlap components and diagnostics. The official package guidance was inspected; **no R model or robust estimator was fitted here**. With three synthetic samples, a bespoke complex model would add a misleading appearance of sophistication. [M12, M13]
+
+For the forthcoming protocol, PROPOSED_PROTOCOL.md offers a conservative primary non-pooling route below five independent components, REML plus explicitly specified conservative HKSJ for otherwise eligible cells, and a separate guarded robust-model option. These cut-offs and modifications are proposed operational safeguards, not established sufficiency guarantees. They need review before freezing; this packet's exact algebra remains useful without adopting them.
+
+## Non-pooling is a deliverable, not a missing analysis
+
+SWiM is a reporting checklist, not proof that a synthesis method is valid. The proposed implementation maps items1–2 to the synthesis key and metric; items3–4 to declared aggregation/selection or non-pooling; items5–6 to heterogeneity and appraisal/uncertainty reasoning; items7–8 to grouped estimates with identifiable contributing samples; item9 to explicit precision, design and transfer limits. We inspected the checklist's two pages, not the full explanation paper. [M09]
+
+A non-pooled cell should show the eligible set, actual contributors, missing uncertainty and omitted-contribution reasons, not only favourable estimates. Magnitude, direction, uncertainty and conditions can be compared without counting significant studies. Absence of a precise pooled result is not proof of no effect. Where direction-only information is all that survives, its lost magnitude and precision must be acknowledged, and dependent rows cannot be treated as independent votes. [M05]
+
+## Implementation sequence and remaining choices
+
+| Order | Intended paths / action | Acceptance connection |
+|---|---|---|
+| 1 | Preserve this additive prepass and fixture under the named research/protocol and tests/fixtures paths. | Actual methods basis and reproducible calculations available without a freeze. |
+| 2 | Reconcile EXTRACTION_CONTRACT with #1 empirical/identity schemas and correction graph; add relevant cross-record tests there. | Independent units, missingness and source identity become representable. |
+| 3 | Pilot the synthesis-key and 14 decisions on heterogeneous, actually read sources; include negative/incompatible cases. | Establish operational effect/eligibility rules without favourable-outcome selection. |
+| 4 | Resolve concrete windows, training/exposure, action deadlines, standardizer and meaningful-effect criteria; review proposed k/df/inference safeguards. | Quantitative estimands and pooling/non-pooling rules ready for freeze. |
+| 5 | Add the accepted clauses to the executable protocol through its owner; log exposure/amendments. Leave original draft labelled. | No silent freeze or overwriting theoretical-method rules. |
+| 6 | Later #9/#10 implement extraction, analysis and verified library-based inference; retain source-fidelity review separately. | Empirical calculations and conclusions trace actual evidence, not this synthetic fixture. |
+
+No existing schema, validator, workflow, RAG authority, programme status or protocol-freeze flag is replaced by this patch. The proposed extra tools/tests are disjoint filenames within #1's broader owned directories and require integrator coordination before publication. The task used one assistant with local self-checks; it is not independent human statistical review. All source and software reading scopes are explicit; no inaccessible text was inferred.
