+# Proposed quantitative protocol clauses
+
+**PROPOSED — NOT FROZEN.** Contribution to MHAC-R002 / #2 assessed against `796151a33ec7a1acc026b239fe5c883312ebf40b`, 2026-10-04. These clauses may be integrated after the heterogeneous pilot and #1 identity/extraction interface are reconciled. They do not freeze the protocol, change the literature cut-off, register a study, claim independent human review, or authorize participants. Method source IDs resolve in SOURCES.md. Thresholds below are explicit conservative project proposals, not universal methodological laws.
+
+## Estimand and synthesis membership
+
+For every proposed quantitative synthesis we will write a synthesis-specific question before inspecting the direction, statistical significance or relative size of its extracted effects. Its key will identify population, task and semantic content, intervention/comparator, training and exposure policy, outcome and response event, time origin/window, treatment of failures/replays/repair, population summary, effect scale, analysis unit and relevant design constraints. A shared cue-family name is insufficient to establish membership. [M01, M03, M08]
+
+A comparison that simultaneously changes sound family, wording, mapping, duration or training will be described as a comparison of interface packages. We will not attribute that package effect to sound style alone. Effects of a manipulated component require a design that isolates that component or an explicitly qualified causal analysis. The project's preferred aesthetic is not an eligibility or weighting criterion.
+
+We will analyse recognition, correct action, latency, training burden, retention and workload as separate outcome families. First-attempt recognition is not appropriate action; perceived urgency and preference are not objective performance. We will not construct a single total-performance score by averaging standardized outcomes. Total interpretive effort remains a multidimensional framing unless a separately justified utility/measurement model is specified before analysis. [M03, M10, M11; original project application]
+
+## Outcome-specific definitions and effect measures
+
+For comparable recognition and action tasks, our proposed primary scale is a marginal risk difference, reported in percentage points. Participant-average accuracy and trial-weighted probability are distinct when trial counts differ; the target weighting will be explicit. A design-valid marginal model contrast or contrast of participant means is preferred to a count that treats repeated trials as independent. Risk ratios and odds ratios may be synthesized in separate compatible cells when those are the identified reported estimands; conditional model odds ratios will not be silently relabelled marginal effects. Detection/false-alarm or sensitivity results remain separate from semantic recognition. [M01; D01–D03]
+
+For action questions, the proposed primary endpoint is appropriate action within the cell's specified deadline and repair policy, with wrong actions, omissions and lateness retained as outcomes. The actual deadline must be justified by the task rather than imposed across unrelated domains. A technical recording loss is missing data, not automatically an action failure. Success after a replay or correction is not first-attempt success. Where source data cannot identify the chosen endpoint, the source remains in the evidence map under its actual endpoint rather than being forced into this synthesis. [M08; project adaptation]
+
+Latency will be identified by clock origin (e.g. sound onset versus sound offset), response event, units, correct/error inclusion and trimming/censoring policy. Arithmetic mean differences in milliseconds, mean-log differences, median differences and other quantiles are not interchangeable. Exponentiating a mean-log difference gives a ratio of geometric means, not a ratio of arithmetic means. Correct-only latency will be explicitly labelled a selected-response estimand and presented alongside accuracy/failure rates; it will not establish an unconditional processing advantage. We will not add an inverse-efficiency composite after seeing an apparent speed–accuracy tradeoff. [M07, M11; D04]
+
+Fixed-dose learning performance, cost to reach a mastery criterion and delayed retention will be analysed separately. Training-to-criterion contrasts must retain non-learners and the training ceiling. Delayed performance level and loss from immediate performance are different estimands. Retention windows must specify exact delay ranges and interim practice/retraining. A cross-interface retention-change estimate requires the appropriate covariance of all contributing measurements or a directly reported design-valid contrast and SE. [M03, M08; D05–D07]
+
+Workload synthesis will retain instrument, version, raw versus weighted scoring, overall versus subscale, direction, task and measurement time. Raw mean differences are preferred for the same instrument/scoring. A standardized mean difference is permitted only for defensibly common constructs measured differently, not to combine workload with accuracy or preference. Weighted TLX, unweighted TLX and individual subscales will not initially be declared equivalent. [M07, M10; D08]
+
+## Effect standardization and design compatibility
+
+We will use raw mean differences when common units have a defensible common interpretation. When standardization is necessary, we will name its denominator and small-sample correction, preserve enough source information to reconstruct it, and use a variance formula appropriate to the design. A paired effect standardized by the SD of within-person differences (d_z) will not be pooled unchanged with between-person outcome-standardized g. The pilot must establish the exact common standardizer before a mixed-design SMD synthesis is enabled; otherwise those results remain separated. [M02, M07]
+
+Parallel and repeated-measures studies are not automatically incompatible, but they must target the same exposure/learning/period question. We will prefer reported design-adjusted contrasts and uncertainty that account for pairing, repeated trials and relevant period/order effects. Training carryover may make an audio crossover fundamentally different from a first-exposure parallel comparison. We will not choose only first-period data in response to a favourable or unfavourable carryover test; any first-period restriction must be prespecified or recorded as an exposed amendment with sensitivity analysis. Non-randomized comparisons will be distinguished and not treated as randomized causal effects. [M01, M02]
+
+## Dependence and duplicate-report handling
+
+Publication, study, experiment, sample and result identities will be reconciled before determining the number of independent contributions. Multiple reports of one result contribute provenance, not extra observations. Complementary outcomes are retained with their common sample identity. A correction supersedes the eligible extraction and invalidates dependent outputs rather than being averaged with the previous value. Unresolved conflicting values or material sample overlap will be flagged and excluded from the affected primary numerical synthesis until a defensible resolution or bounded sensitivity is available. [M06; #1 interface]
+
+Our default small-corpus strategy is one prespecified effect per independent sample and synthesis cell, or an explicitly justified within-sample aggregate. The same sample may contribute to distinct separately analysed outcome families, but those families do not become independent confirmations; a joint comparison requires cross-outcome dependence handling. Selection follows the declared task/outcome/time/exemplar hierarchy and design validity, not significance, magnitude or apparent precision. Other eligible estimates remain visible in the non-pooled display. We will aggregate only measurements that estimate a declared common target or a deliberately defined mixture, with weights stated before results selection. Within-sample variance is a'Va, not the average of squared SEs divided by an invented independent count. [M03, M12]
+
+For independent arms sharing a control, covariance of two raw mean contrasts is the variance of that shared control mean, with signs determined by the contrast vectors. When conditions share participants, all relevant condition covariances are needed. All pairwise contrasts from three conditions are not three independent pieces of information; we will use a nonredundant basis or a valid aggregate. Combining genuinely different cue families merely to eliminate dependence changes the question and is not permitted. Approximate shared-control splitting will not be the default and will not be used to repair repeated-measures designs. [M02; explicit covariance derivation in FINDINGS.md]
+
+## Missing uncertainty, correlations and observations
+
+We will distinguish uncertainty not reported, not recoverable, and genuinely zero by design; missing SD/SE will never be replaced by zero. Valid SE recovery may use a CI with its confidence level and degrees of freedom, a test for the actual contrast or SD of paired differences. An omnibus ANOVA p-value, a threshold such as p<.05 or an unlabelled error bar is insufficient. Reported adjusted uncertainty will not be adjusted for pairing a second time. [M01, M02]
+
+Person-level within-condition correlations and correlations between estimated contrasts are different quantities. The former can reduce the variance of a difference; positive covariance between contrasts increases the variance of their average. Each required covariance will have a source, direct calculation or explicitly labelled assumption. Where it is unavailable, we will first use a reported paired/model contrast or existing accessible supplementary data, then examine justified sensitivity ranges and matrix feasibility. No single assumed correlation, including 0 or 0.5, will be presented as observed. A sensitivity grid is not a confidence interval for an unknown correlation. [M02, M12; derivation]
+
+For paired binary data we will use the joint table or valid marginal contrast uncertainty; where only margins exist, feasible joint-table bounds may identify a variance range. Marginal counts alone generally do not identify paired precision. Borrowed SDs or correlations require an identified comparable source, an exclusion sensitivity and explicit assumption provenance. Persistent lack of a defensible variance precludes inverse-variance pooling but does not remove the source from the evidence map. Sparse or all-zero events require a prespecified suitable method or non-pooling, not a universal arbitrary continuity correction. [M01; synthetic binary derivation]
+
+We will record the number and reason for missing participants/trials and distinguish this from omissions, timeouts and training failure. Correct-only analysis does not define a population that would succeed under both interfaces. Errors may be competing outcomes rather than independently censored observations; time-to-correct analyses require their own event and censoring assumptions. We will not infer those assumptions from the presence of a survival-model label. [M08; original HCI adaptation]
+
+## Across-sample pooling and statistical inference
+
+Compatibility, independent information, identified uncertainty and bias/coverage will determine eligibility before any heterogeneity test. A random-effects model cannot repair different constructs or biased outcome selection. For the primary protocol we propose non-pooled presentation for cells with fewer than five independent sample/overlap components; this is a conservative project safeguard, not a claim that five guarantees adequacy. A separately justified finite-target average may be shown as such, without population-generalization language. [M03–M05]
+
+For eligible cells with at least five independent contributions, the proposed default is a random-effects mean with REML heterogeneity estimation and a conservative modified HKSJ interval, using t(k-1) and inflating the conventional inverse-total-variance SE by sqrt(max(1,q)), where q = sum[w_i (y_i - mu_hat)^2]/(k-1), w_i = 1/(v_i + tau_hat^2), and mu_hat = sum(w_i y_i)/sum(w_i). We will report k, the exact implementation/version and weighting, individual estimates, estimated heterogeneity and sensitivity to the unmodified HKSJ interval and dependence assumptions. This specification must receive known-answer implementation tests before empirical use; it is not implemented by the small algebraic fixture here. Prediction intervals will be withheld or strongly qualified where between-study prediction is poorly identified. No heterogeneity p-value determines whether to switch models. [M04, M15; adoption of the conservative modification is a proposed project choice]
+
+A future multivariate/multilevel analysis must specify the sampling covariance V separately from the random effects for variation in true effects. Robust variance inference is a possible supplement, not a way to ignore duplicate identities or incompatible endpoints. We propose considering CR2/Satterthwaite inference only with at least ten independent overlap components, coefficient-specific df of at least four, and acceptable leverage/identifiability diagnostics; this is an intentionally conservative screening rule, not a universal validity threshold. Failing it triggers aggregation/selection or non-pooling. The three-sample rehearsal does not estimate robust or random-effects models. [M12, M13]
+
+## Multiplicity, sensitivity and non-pooled synthesis
+
+We will retain the declared primary outcome and cue/time hierarchy, all eligible results, and any deviations. Intervals are marginal descriptive intervals unless a confirmatory family and simultaneous inference method were prespecified. No universal superiority claim will be inferred from several unadjusted favourable contrasts. Confirmatory multiple-comparison families and adjustments, if needed, will be fixed before those tests; this evidence-map prepass does not create such a family. Moderator, publication-bias and exploratory subgroup analyses require adequate independent data and a separate justified specification. [M03; project policy]
+
+When pooling is not justified, we will display each independent sample with its linked contrasts, estimate, uncertainty or explicit missing-uncertainty label, design, training/delay and risk-of-bias/transfer caveats. We will explain the grouping, prioritization and non-pooling reason. We will compare magnitude and uncertainty without counting statistical significance as votes, presenting a silent overall diamond or interpreting inconclusiveness as no effect. Dependent rows are not independent votes. Distributional summaries, if used, will explicitly describe their weighting/unit and will not be called a pooled mean. [M05, M09]
+
+Reasons will be coded as one or more of: `CONSTRUCT`, `TASK_OR_EXPOSURE`, `CLOCK_OR_FAILURE_POLICY`, `EFFECT_SCALE`, `DESIGN_OR_CARRYOVER`, `UNKNOWN_VARIANCE`, `UNKNOWN_DEPENDENCE`, `DUPLICATE_CONFLICT`, `SPARSE_INDEPENDENT_INFORMATION`, `SELECTION_OR_BIAS`, `INSUFFICIENT_COVERAGE`. Correlation sensitivity, missingness sensitivity and design/appraisal exclusions will be reported separately. We will not choose the correlation or analytic subset that produces a preferred conclusion.
+
+## Remaining integration choices before any freeze
+
+The pilot must settle concrete population/task cells, exposure levels, retention windows, outcome-specific deadlines, acceptable replay/repair policies, useful effect thresholds and the final common SMD standardizer where necessary. The proposed k/df safeguards and modified-HKSJ option require statistical review and versioned implementation tests. #1 must supply accepted identity/overlap/correction fields; independent overlap components require source-grounded judgment, not inferred publication counts. No primary outcome hierarchy or methodological amendment is to be silently installed by applying this additive prepass patch.
